import asyncio, edge_tts, subprocess, os
CH=[
"Okay, so here's the thing that shouldn't be true. You've got a twenty-four gigabyte card. You want the biggest model it'll hold. So you go looking for the biggest one you can find, and you land on a thirty billion parameter model. Then somebody in the comments tells you the smarter buy is a twenty-seven billion parameter model, because the thirty B won't fit. Except it does fit. It fits better. On the same card, the thirty billion parameter model uses less memory, holds more context, and generates tokens faster than the twenty-seven billion one. Same quantization family, same inference engine, same GPU. And it's not a trick. It's three measurable things: the file size, the cache, and the context window. Once you see it, you can't unsee it. So in this one we put Muse Glimmer thirty B against Qwen three point eight twenty-seven B, on the same cards, at the same context lengths, and we only look at the numbers that decide whether it runs.",
"The rule everyone repeats is simple. More parameters means more memory. Bigger model, bigger card. And it's a good rule, because it's usually true. Muse Glimmer is a dense model with twenty-seven point eight five billion active parameters. Qwen three point eight twenty-seven B is twenty-seven point three two billion parameters. So on paper these two are the same size class. Nine hundred million parameters apart, which is nothing. But the rule hides the thing that actually fills your VRAM. Your card doesn't store parameters. It stores a quantized file, and it stores a cache, and the cache grows with every token you give it. So let's do the part nobody puts in the spec sheet.",
"First, the file. At four-bit, Muse Glimmer thirty B is fourteen point seven eight gigabytes. Qwen three point eight twenty-seven B is sixteen point six eight gigabytes. Read that again. The thirty billion parameter model is almost two gigabytes smaller than the twenty-seven billion one. And that's not magic, and it's not compression cheating. Muse Glimmer was measured at four-bit medium. Qwen was measured at four-bit small. Different quantization recipes, and the file size follows the recipe, not the parameter count. That's the first honest caveat, and we'll come back to it. Second, the cache. Every token in your context writes a key matrix and a value matrix into memory. Those matrices don't care how many parameters the model has. They care how many layers it has, how many heads, and how long your context is. So you get a situation where the bigger model, in the smaller file, with the leaner cache, fits a longer conversation into the same twenty-four gigabytes.",
"Here's measured VRAM. Not estimated, not calculated from a formula. Measured. At four K context, Muse Glimmer sits at sixteen gigabytes. Qwen is at eighteen. At thirty-two K, Glimmer is still sixteen. Qwen is at twenty. At sixty-four K, Glimmer is seventeen. Qwen is twenty-two. At one twenty-eight K, Glimmer is eighteen. Qwen is twenty-six. And at two hundred fifty-six K, Glimmer peaks at twenty gigabytes. Qwen needs thirty-four. So on a twenty-four gigabyte card, Qwen three point eight holds sixty-four K of context with about two gigabytes left over. Muse Glimmer holds two hundred fifty-six K, the entire window it supports, with four gigabytes still spare. That's the whole video in one line. Same card. Four times the context.",
"Now the part that made me check the numbers twice. On an RTX three thousand ninety, Muse Glimmer generates forty-five point three tokens a second at four K. Qwen does forty point three. Close. Not embarrassing. At sixty-four K, Glimmer holds forty-two point four. Qwen drops to thirty-three point nine. At one twenty-eight K, Glimmer is still doing thirty-eight point one. And Qwen isn't in that test, because on a twenty-four gigabyte card, Qwen three point eight at one twenty-eight K isn't practical. That's the benchmark's own wording, not my opinion. On a five-oh-ninety it's the same story with more headroom. Glimmer at four K: eighty-three point two. Qwen: seventy-four point eight. At one twenty-eight K, Glimmer is at seventy-five point eight, and Qwen has fallen to twenty-two point eight. And here's the detail that actually surprised me. Qwen three point eight isn't just slower than Glimmer. At sixty-four K on a five-oh-ninety, Qwen three point six, the previous generation, is doing sixty-three point six tokens a second, against three point eight's twenty-six point two. The older Qwen is more than twice as fast at long context as the newer one. So newer doesn't mean faster, and more parameters doesn't mean more memory. The spec sheet is lying to you by omission.",
"Three honest caveats, because this is where most videos stop. One. The quantization levels are different. Glimmer was measured at four-bit medium, Qwen at four-bit small. That's not a perfectly fair fight, and anyone telling you it is is selling you something. Two. These benchmarks measure hardware behaviour, not intelligence. The source is explicit: VRAM, context scaling, prompt processing, token generation. Not reasoning quality. Not coding quality. A model that runs fast and fits can still be the wrong model for you. Three. The agentic claims around Glimmer are not independently benchmarked. Tool use, task accuracy, reliability, unconfirmed. Treat them as marketing until you test them yourself. What is confirmed: the file size, the VRAM curve, and the token rates. Those are measurements, and they hold.",
"So if you're on twenty-four gigabytes and you want long context, Muse Glimmer thirty B is the one that fits. Two hundred fifty-six K in twenty gigabytes, at thirty-five to forty-five tokens a second on a three thousand ninety. If you specifically need Qwen's behaviour, then sixty-four K is your realistic ceiling on that card, and you should plan around it instead of discovering it at token ninety thousand. And if you're on a five-oh-ninety, you're not buying context anymore, because twenty gigabytes already covers Glimmer's whole window. You're buying speed. Eighty-three tokens a second at four K, sixty-nine at two hundred fifty-six K. Notice what none of those decisions were based on. Parameter count. Not once.",
"So stop shopping by parameter count. Shop by the file, the cache, and the context you actually use. Those three numbers are on the page. The parameter count tells you the least. That's the forensic part. See you in the next one.",
]
async def main():
    tot=0; words=0
    for i,t in enumerate(CH,1):
        c=edge_tts.Communicate(t,"en-US-AndrewNeural",rate="+8%")
        buf=bytearray()
        async for ch in c.stream():
            if ch["type"]=="audio": buf+=ch["data"]
        p=f"_e{i}.mp3"; open(p,"wb").write(bytes(buf))
        d=float(subprocess.run(["ffprobe","-v","error","-show_entries","format=duration","-of","csv=p=0",p],
              capture_output=True,text=True).stdout.strip())
        os.remove(p); tot+=d; words+=len(t.split())
        print(f"  CH{i}: {d:6.2f}s  {len(t.split()):4d} words")
    gaps=0.30*7; tail=1.0
    print(f"\n  speech {tot:.1f}s + {7} gaps {gaps:.1f}s + tail {tail}s")
    print(f"  RUNTIME {tot+gaps+tail:.1f}s  =  {(tot+gaps+tail)/60:.2f} min")
    print(f"  words {words}  wpm {words/(tot/60):.0f}")
    print(f"  reveals needed at 2.0s max: {int((tot+gaps+tail)/1.4)} approx")
asyncio.run(main())
